hing  like a 63 increase it's a much bigger  increase than what you see represented  in the relative size of the bars  so  that's misleading but what's even worse  is if you look at the neymar jerseys  that were sold that's all the way down  there at 10k which is  about two percent of 520k  but you'll notice this bar is about  maybe one quarter of the size of this  520k bar  uh when really it should be closer to  like two percent of the size of the bar  so  again there's all kinds of things that  are wrong with this graph  and if you take a graph that under to  the untrained eye  i'm sure it looks a lot less fancy but  if you actually make the bars uh  geometrically proportional to the data  that's being represented in them you'll  see here's the 850k bar then 520k is  again this one is about 63 bigger  and then you've got this super tiny 10k  bar down here again this may look less  fancy  but it's but it's objectively a much  better graph  now moving on to principle number two  clear detailed and thorough labeling  should be used to defeat graphical  distortion and ambiguity write out  explanations of the data on the graphic  itself and  as necessary you can label important  events in the data  so this one is pretty straightforward  and self-explanatory but it could be  helpful to look at a bad example  so if we look at this graphic here  there's again all kinds of things that  are wrong with it  so part of labeling a graph includes  putting some kind of title on it  typically there's no title on here and  there's also no x and y labels so that's  that's problematic so i don't really  know what anything here is supposed to  represent  and this is something that you can run  into if you're using something like  ggplot2 and r to create a graph because  often what it'll do is it'll generate  your bars in order of the factors as  they appear in the data and that can get  problematic because what you see here is  it goes two and then one dash terrible  so the one represents a terrible but we  don't know what the two  represents does that correspond to some  kind of um of verbal kind of category  then it's three okay and four again no  category for it  five fine and dandy and then n a  so you want some kind of clear labeling  again on the axis here and then you want  these to be a lot more clean and  consistent and in order so what that  often will look like again if you're  using something like ggplot2 you'll have  to go in and actually  order the factors in the in the order  that you want uh but then also change  the labels on them but if you do that  it's it's really going to make the graph  tell a much much better story than this  does  all right now the third principle of  graphical integrity is show data  variation not design variation  and this is getting back to the  principle of the data ink ratio where as  much ink as possible on your graph  should be used to represent the data and  nothing else  and specifically when you see variation  on the graph  that should completely be just to  represent variation in the underlying  data so let's look at a bad example  where this where this is not put into  practice  so  when i looked at this graph at first it  took me way too long to figure out what  was actually going on on it  essentially what it's doing is  it's comparing opec oil prices over time  but it's doing it at first on a yearly  scale so 1973 to 1978  and then it drills down into the year  1979  to look at it by quarter and i guess the  intention with this graph was to  illustrate that it was really  skyrocketing in the year 1979 and in  fact uh so much that you could look at  it that what would normally be a year's  worth of variation showed up uh in in  quarters  but it's so hard to figure out what's  actually going on in this graph because  you have all this ink that's going on  over here and uh going on here uh above  these bars here where again it does  nothing to actually  illustrate anything uh i'm looking at  t